repository-code: 'https://github.com/LukasK13/fuel-cell-simulator'
abstract: >-
  Long-term operation of proton exchange membrane (PEM) fuel
  cells requires precise monitoring to detect and prevent
  harmful anomalies. Cell voltage is a sensitive indicator
  for the fuel cell’s state of health. However, its
  dependency on fuel cell operating conditions impedes the
  detection of anomalies. To overcome this limitation, we
  present a machine learning driven foundation model for
  probabilistic fuel cell voltage prediction. The foundation
  model can be fitted to new fuel cell designs using
  transfer learning for anomaly detection. Since the
  availability and variance in real fuel cell test data sets
  is very limited, a physics-based fuel cell simulation
  model is used to generate 30,000 data points to train the
  machine learning model. To improve the prediction accuracy
  of the foundation model, additional steps such as feature
  engineering and target balancing are introduced and their
  individual effects are analyzed. The performance of the
  pre-trained foundation model is then tested by transfer
  learning the foundation model using real measurement data
  from two different automotive fuel cell designs. It is
  shown that with sufficient training data, the machine
  learning model outperforms the physics-based simulation
  model by an RMSE improvement of up to 37% and respectively
  by 61% on the two test data sets.
